WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:05.517
[MUSIC]

00:00:05.517 --> 00:00:12.045
It's summer 2018, I'm sitting at my
desk and one of my clients walks in.

00:00:12.045 --> 00:00:15.874
He drops his bag on the floor and
turns to the others in the office,

00:00:15.874 --> 00:00:18.519
asking what they think
of the latest headline

00:00:18.519 --> 00:00:22.082
about Brett Kavanaugh's
nomination to the Supreme Court.

00:00:22.082 --> 00:00:27.960
As the ten men around me casually debate
whether he had actually assaulted Dr.

00:00:27.960 --> 00:00:30.908
Blasey Ford, I feel my chest tighten.

00:00:30.908 --> 00:00:36.134
I see them speaking, but their voices
drop off and I can't hear anything.

00:00:36.134 --> 00:00:39.604
I stumble to the bathroom
in search of privacy.

00:00:39.604 --> 00:00:43.682
As the memories begin to flood my brain,
I bury my face in my palms.

00:00:43.682 --> 00:00:45.925
I can't stop them.

00:00:45.925 --> 00:00:48.394
Suddenly, someone walks
into the bathroom and

00:00:48.394 --> 00:00:51.118
the noise is enough to jolt
me back into the present.

00:00:51.118 --> 00:00:56.666
I take a deep shaky breath, and walk
back to my desk as if nothing happened.

00:00:56.666 --> 00:01:00.234
These episodes continued and
became unbearable.

00:01:00.234 --> 00:01:01.667
I knew I needed help, and

00:01:01.667 --> 00:01:05.777
finally made an appointment with
a therapist who would change my life.

00:01:05.777 --> 00:01:10.629
She helped me see that these were
PTSD-like flashbacks common among trauma

00:01:10.629 --> 00:01:11.476
survivors.

00:01:11.476 --> 00:01:15.162
I had thought these were
work-related anxiety attacks.

00:01:15.162 --> 00:01:19.953
I had no idea and I hadn't even realized
that my client's comments where triggering

00:01:19.953 --> 00:01:23.071
flashbacks to my own experience
with sexual assault.

00:01:23.071 --> 00:01:28.143
An experience I'd suppressed for
years, now rearing its head.

00:01:28.143 --> 00:01:32.484
It was clear I would need to make time for
consistent regular therapy to address

00:01:32.484 --> 00:01:35.130
these flashbacks and
their underlying cause.

00:01:35.130 --> 00:01:39.081
Unfortunately, the only time
available in my therapist's

00:01:39.081 --> 00:01:42.180
practice was Tuesday mornings at 9:30 AM,

00:01:42.180 --> 00:01:47.067
right in the middle of the weekly update
to our clients that I usually lead.

00:01:47.067 --> 00:01:49.929
I dreaded bringing this up with my boss.

00:01:49.929 --> 00:01:53.800
I didn't want him to think I was weak
by disclosing that I was dealing with

00:01:53.800 --> 00:01:55.079
a mental health issue.

00:01:55.079 --> 00:01:58.434
I decided to frame it vaguely
around a medical issue.

00:01:58.434 --> 00:02:00.877
Surely he'd understand that.

00:02:00.877 --> 00:02:03.437
I approached him one day
on our way out of work,

00:02:03.437 --> 00:02:07.586
stumbling over my words as I tried to
blurt them out as quickly as possible.

00:02:07.586 --> 00:02:10.083
I need to see a doctor weekly for
treatment, and

00:02:10.083 --> 00:02:12.838
the only time available
is Tuesdays at 9:30 AM.

00:02:12.838 --> 00:02:16.123
I know that conflicts with our
weekly client meetings, but

00:02:16.123 --> 00:02:19.220
this is a priority for me and
I'm hoping you can help me.

00:02:19.220 --> 00:02:20.973
He looks concerned.

00:02:20.973 --> 00:02:22.884
Is this a serious medical issue?

00:02:22.884 --> 00:02:26.627
Would extended medical
leave be a better option?

00:02:26.627 --> 00:02:30.963
No, now he actually thought I
had a serious medical condition.

00:02:30.963 --> 00:02:33.991
I decided to backtrack and
tell him the truth.

00:02:33.991 --> 00:02:36.314
No, no, it's nothing serious.

00:02:36.314 --> 00:02:38.637
Well, it is serious.

00:02:38.637 --> 00:02:42.921
I'm going through a tough time and
I need to see a therapist weekly.

00:02:42.921 --> 00:02:48.765
He nodded slowly, are there any other days
of the week you could see your therapist?

00:02:48.765 --> 00:02:51.509
Could you see them before or
after working hours?

00:02:51.509 --> 00:02:55.873
I shook my head, no,
this is the only time available.

00:02:55.873 --> 00:02:58.736
Maybe you could go every other week.

00:02:58.736 --> 00:03:03.724
No, I need to see this person every week,
that's non-negotiable.

00:03:03.724 --> 00:03:07.429
He looked at me for a beat and
I saw something shift in his face.

00:03:07.429 --> 00:03:12.195
You know what, do what you have to do,
we'll make it work.

00:03:12.195 --> 00:03:16.587
As I reflect back on this experience, it
makes me sad to realize that the only way

00:03:16.587 --> 00:03:21.260
I thought my mental health would be taken
seriously was to claim a physical illness.

00:03:21.260 --> 00:03:24.983
If in 2018 I'd torn my ACL and
needed physical therapy,

00:03:24.983 --> 00:03:27.685
I would have had no
problem going to my boss.

00:03:27.685 --> 00:03:32.672
So why did it feel so much harder
to share my mental health needs?

00:03:32.672 --> 00:03:36.316
One year later, I myself was
leading a team on a high profile,

00:03:36.316 --> 00:03:39.486
high stress project with
one of our largest clients.

00:03:39.486 --> 00:03:41.693
And I could tell something was wrong.

00:03:41.693 --> 00:03:46.435
One of the associates on my team, we'll
call her Rachel, usually very focused and

00:03:46.435 --> 00:03:48.888
high-performing, seemed distracted.

00:03:48.888 --> 00:03:50.664
I checked in with her one-on-one and

00:03:50.664 --> 00:03:53.326
she shared that she was
struggling with depression, but

00:03:53.326 --> 00:03:56.892
unable to consistently see her therapist
because of our travel schedules.

00:03:56.892 --> 00:04:01.007
As consultants, we typically traveled
to be on site, at our clients,

00:04:01.007 --> 00:04:04.308
Monday to Thursday,
spending three nights on the road.

00:04:04.308 --> 00:04:07.078
My heart ached for
Rachel when she told me this.

00:04:07.078 --> 00:04:10.136
I thought back to my moment
of crisis here earlier and

00:04:10.136 --> 00:04:12.929
I wanted to help her get
the support she needed.

00:04:12.929 --> 00:04:16.198
But again,
I was nervous to bring it up with my boss.

00:04:16.198 --> 00:04:20.043
I was concerned he might think that I and
the team couldn't handle the pressures of

00:04:20.043 --> 00:04:22.956
the job, which we'd signed up for
knowing involved travel.

00:04:22.956 --> 00:04:26.555
And I was up for a promotion, and
I was worried my manager might doubt my

00:04:26.555 --> 00:04:30.341
leadership abilities if I didn't tell
Rachel, no, we have to be there.

00:04:30.341 --> 00:04:32.732
This is what the client wants.

00:04:32.732 --> 00:04:34.791
But I wasn't willing to tell her no,

00:04:34.791 --> 00:04:37.457
I wanted to help her get
the support she needed.

00:04:37.457 --> 00:04:41.053
I knew firsthand the impact
that an hour a week with my

00:04:41.053 --> 00:04:45.895
therapist had made on my mental health,
and I wanted the same for her.

00:04:45.895 --> 00:04:47.326
I set up time with my boss and

00:04:47.326 --> 00:04:50.760
I told him the team wanted to reduce
our number of days on the road.

00:04:50.760 --> 00:04:56.085
He initially pushed back saying, the
clients aren't paying us to sit at home.

00:04:56.085 --> 00:04:59.991
But eventually, after I emphasized
the physical and mental toll this

00:04:59.991 --> 00:05:04.374
was taking on the team, he agreed to try
out a three day a week travel schedule.

00:05:04.374 --> 00:05:09.624
The team stuck with it, and Rachel was
able to see her therapist every Thursday.

00:05:09.624 --> 00:05:14.622
Now, I don't claim to be the model
of effective management, after all,

00:05:14.622 --> 00:05:16.246
this was before my MBA.

00:05:16.246 --> 00:05:17.815
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; But

00:05:17.815 --> 00:05:22.582
the impact that my personal experience had
had on me allowed me to empathize with

00:05:22.582 --> 00:05:25.372
Rachel and
help her get the support she needs.

00:05:25.372 --> 00:05:28.741
I recognize not every work
environment will be this flexible.

00:05:28.741 --> 00:05:33.549
But in the white collar, salaried work
environments many of us will go into after

00:05:33.549 --> 00:05:38.286
we graduate, why is it so hard to imagine
a work environment where employees can

00:05:38.286 --> 00:05:43.049
prioritize their mental health, even if
it's just an hour a week for therapy?

00:05:43.049 --> 00:05:44.966
My view is that making space for

00:05:44.966 --> 00:05:50.211
mental health at work is hard because the
stigma around mental health still exists.

00:05:50.211 --> 00:05:54.127
80% of employees reported
stigma in the workplace about

00:05:54.127 --> 00:05:57.738
mental health in a recent
survey from April 2021.

00:05:57.738 --> 00:06:01.433
Which is astounding when you
realize that in reality,

00:06:01.433 --> 00:06:05.384
20% of American adults
struggle with mental illness.

00:06:05.384 --> 00:06:07.028
Let that sink in.

00:06:07.028 --> 00:06:11.928
One in five American adults you walk by on
the street, see in the grocery store, and

00:06:11.928 --> 00:06:13.958
who are even sitting in this room,

00:06:13.958 --> 00:06:17.399
are struggling with their
mental health on a daily basis.

00:06:17.399 --> 00:06:22.117
And to make matters worse, less than half
of those people get the treatment or

00:06:22.117 --> 00:06:23.358
support they need.

00:06:23.358 --> 00:06:28.095
To put it into context, one in five
Americans has been diagnosed with

00:06:28.095 --> 00:06:32.433
COVID-19 to date,
the same prevalence as mental illness.

00:06:32.433 --> 00:06:37.127
Mental health is the pandemic
we're not talking about.

00:06:37.127 --> 00:06:40.763
Our deprioritization of mental
health isn't a new challenge.

00:06:40.763 --> 00:06:45.693
However, we have structurally valued
mental health to a lesser degree

00:06:45.693 --> 00:06:47.344
than physical health.

00:06:47.344 --> 00:06:51.687
Mental health benefits are less likely to
be covered by health insurance plans, and

00:06:51.687 --> 00:06:54.028
are reimbursed at lower
rates when they are.

00:06:54.028 --> 00:06:58.561
The number of federally funded
residency positions in psychiatry has

00:06:58.561 --> 00:07:03.245
remained flat since the 1990s,
even as the population has grown and

00:07:03.245 --> 00:07:06.347
demand for
mental health services has soared.

00:07:06.347 --> 00:07:10.981
These are just a few of the systemic
barriers preventing access to affordable

00:07:10.981 --> 00:07:12.557
mental health services.

00:07:12.557 --> 00:07:17.148
Today, I want to focus on what we
all can do in our everyday lives to

00:07:17.148 --> 00:07:20.637
break down the stigma
around mental health, and

00:07:20.637 --> 00:07:24.654
help people take that first
step towards seeking care.

00:07:24.654 --> 00:07:28.875
I believe we can accomplish this by
shifting our perspective around mental

00:07:28.875 --> 00:07:32.164
health to be equally as important
as our physical health.

00:07:32.164 --> 00:07:36.929
Prevention, trauma, and
recovery exist in both realms.

00:07:36.929 --> 00:07:40.559
Why should we think differently of either?

00:07:40.559 --> 00:07:44.573
We can start by normalizing mental health
at home, in our private lives, and

00:07:44.573 --> 00:07:48.918
in conversations with friends and family,
and also in our public lives, at work.

00:07:48.918 --> 00:07:51.954
It's at work where I see
the greatest potential for

00:07:51.954 --> 00:07:54.361
a shift in stigma around mental health.

00:07:54.361 --> 00:07:58.852
Now, much has been written about what
corporations can do at an organizational

00:07:58.852 --> 00:08:01.271
level to support employee mental health.

00:08:01.271 --> 00:08:05.690
I want to focus on what we all can do
in our everyday interactions at work to

00:08:05.690 --> 00:08:08.648
support those struggling
with mental health.

00:08:08.648 --> 00:08:11.192
We can start by checking
in on our people and

00:08:11.192 --> 00:08:14.739
asking those on our teams what
they need to be at their best.

00:08:14.739 --> 00:08:17.590
Hear your employees and
respect their needs,

00:08:17.590 --> 00:08:22.352
without judging whether their needs
are worth investing time into therapy.

00:08:22.352 --> 00:08:26.680
Ask yourself if an employee or colleague
of yours needed physical therapy,

00:08:26.680 --> 00:08:28.582
would you respond differently?

00:08:28.582 --> 00:08:32.777
And don't underestimate your role
in creating a supportive work

00:08:32.777 --> 00:08:36.676
environment through small
changes to your daily schedule.

00:08:36.676 --> 00:08:40.452
I challenge you to ask these questions
of yourselves to better support those

00:08:40.452 --> 00:08:42.466
struggling with mental health at work.

00:08:42.466 --> 00:08:46.141
Can you be the one to role model
what it looks like to make space for

00:08:46.141 --> 00:08:47.514
mental health at work?

00:08:47.514 --> 00:08:53.828
It might make a world of a difference to
someone on your team, as it did for me.

00:08:53.828 --> 00:08:57.729
I still see a psychologist every week,
four years later, and

00:08:57.729 --> 00:09:02.015
I am still working through the effects
of the trauma I experienced.

00:09:02.015 --> 00:09:06.978
But the impact of managers who
respected the boundaries I set at work

00:09:06.978 --> 00:09:08.728
cannot be overstated.

00:09:08.728 --> 00:09:13.891
They took my mental health seriously and
respected my needs when I made them known.

00:09:13.891 --> 00:09:17.371
My team was fine without me at
that weekly client meeting.

00:09:17.371 --> 00:09:21.704
All I needed was a compassionate manager
who was willing to step in for me.

00:09:21.704 --> 00:09:26.073
I challenge you to be that compassionate
manager for those on your teams and

00:09:26.073 --> 00:09:27.262
in your workplace.

00:09:27.262 --> 00:09:28.156
Thank you.

00:09:28.156 --> 00:09:30.436
&gt;&gt; [APPLAUSE]

00:09:30.436 --> 00:09:34.530
[MUSIC]

